<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 59 BCE – c. 17 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Titus Livius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Livy (Titus Livius) was a Roman historian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Patavium</a> (Padua) who wrote Ab Urbe Condita, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from its foundation in 142 books, of which 35 survive. Writing under Augustus, he is a principal source for early Rome, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> and Rome's wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, although he relied on earlier annalists and used the past to draw moral lessons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livy.jpg" alt="Livy" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acediscovery · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus-Livius-Austrian-Parliament-Building.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Livy was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Patavium</a>, modern Padua, probably in 59 BCE. His city was then the second wealthiest on the Italian peninsula, and it was known for conservative values in morality and politics. His teenage years fell in the civil wars of the 40s BCE. When the governor Asinius Pollio tried to bring Patavium over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, the wealthy citizens refused money and arms and pledged their allegiance to the Senate. The wars probably kept Livy from a higher education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Pollio later mocked his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for its patavinity, the provincialisms of Patavium.</p><p>Livy probably went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 30s BCE but never became a senator or held office. Mistakes in his work on military matters suggest that he never served in the army. He was educated in philosophy and rhetoric, and had the means to live independently. He knew the emperor Augustus and encouraged the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> to write history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ab Urbe Condita, told the story of the city from the legends before its traditional founding in 753 BCE through the reign of Augustus. He began it when already past his youth, probably at 33, and worked on it until he left Rome for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in old age. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> he is one of the main accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Because he wrote under Augustus, his history stresses the triumphs of Rome and embellishes Roman heroism, in order to support the new form of government. Since he mostly described events hundreds of years old, its historical value has been questioned, although many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> came to believe his account.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Livy died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Patavium</a> in 17 CE. The ancient evidence for his dates is a chronicle of Eusebius, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>'s Latin version, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> disagree, so that no standard dates exist for him; one manuscript puts his birth in 57 BCE.</p><p>His history was in high demand from its publication. Later writers such as Eutropius, Florus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a> drew on it. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> readers used summaries, since copying the whole was costly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were lost. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> large sums changed hands for Livian manuscripts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and Pope Nicholas V searched for the missing books. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> presented his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a> as a commentary on the history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Livy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through Polybius, <b>Livy</b> and Plutarch's lives of Demetrius, Aratus and Aemilius Paullus, the dynasty became for later readers a classic example of Hellenistic kingship and of its defeat by Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Etruriae, held assemblies annually at the Fanum Voltumnae, the shrine of the god Voltumna at Volsinii, and there, <b>Livy</b> says, chose a leader to represent the league, the zilath mechl rasnal, 'magistrate of the Etruscan people', whom modern scholars see as largely a ceremonial head rather than a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman authors, including <b>Livy</b>, Cicero and Varro, frequently refer to the Etrusca Disciplina, supposedly a collection of sacred books that gave instructions for communicating with the gods through natural signs, haruspicy and augury.</blockquote><a href="/religion/etruscan-religion">Etruscan religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Livy</b> recorded the prodigies of the war with Hannibal, among them a phantom navy in the sky, and the self-sacrifice of Decius Mus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Virgil’s Aeneid gave Rome a national epic, Horace wrote odes and satires, Ovid retold the myths of transformation in the Metamorphoses, and <b>Livy</b> wrote a history of Rome from its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Livy</b> tells that when the Romans protested at the weights used for their ransom, Brennus threw his sword onto the scales with the words “Woe to the vanquished”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Patavium. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=-10" aria-label="Padua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Livy"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 6171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 6171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=-10">What was here in 10 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…anon how they sunk more and more, then began to fall headlong, until he reaches the present times, when we can neither endure our vices, nor their remedies.&#8221;</p><footer>History of Rome, preface &#183; tr. D. Spillan, 1849 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19725/pg19725-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This indulgence is conceded to antiquity, that by blending things human with divine, it may make the origin of cities appear more venerable…&#8221;</p><footer>History of Rome, preface &#183; tr. D. Spillan, 1849 &#183; on the legends of Rome's founding &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19725/pg19725-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But either a fond partiality for the task I have undertaken deceives me, or there never was any state either greater, or more moral, or richer in good examples, nor one into which luxury and avarice made their entrance so late…&#8221;</p><footer>History of Rome, preface &#183; tr. D. Spillan, 1849 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19725/pg19725-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
